<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  CS625 shielding-module insertion scene.

  The fixture model is expressed in the SolidWorks ASSEMBLY frame, and the
  seated module pose was measured in that same frame, so the assembly frame is
  placed here once and both parts keep their exported coordinates.  Only the
  assembly-to-world transform below is a choice; everything else is measured.

  Three facts fix that transform, and each came from the geometry after an
  earlier reading of the same numbers gave the wrong answer:

    - The arm's zero pose reaches along -X (FK puts the wrist origin at
      (-1374, 0, 401) mm), so the task belongs at -X, in front of the robot.
    - The module's handles sit on its own -Y face, which maps to assembly +X, and
      they must face the robot.  With the fixture at -X that means assembly +X
      goes to world +X.
    - The fixture's WIDE end is the base, and it is at assembly +Z.  Measured
      from the mesh, the X and Y spans run from 20.8 mm and 386 mm at assembly
      Z -260..-230 up to 200 mm and the full 745 mm at Z +190..+250.  So assembly
      +Z must point down.

  Those give R = Rx(180 deg) * Ry(+5.004 deg).  The Ry term levels the part: the
  fixture as modelled is tilted 5.004 deg about its own Y axis, which the two
  1878 cm^2 side faces pin down because they are exactly normal to Y while the
  947 cm^2 top plate is 5.004 deg off horizontal.  That is a rigid tilt of the
  whole part, not a modelling error.  Smaller faces stay off-axis, which a welded
  assembly would be expected to have.

  Assembly origin at world (-0.720, 0.000, 0.262063), RPY (180 deg, 5.004 deg, 0):
    fixture  world X [-0.7382, -0.5382]  Y [-0.3725, 0.3725]  Z [0.0000, 0.5077] m
    module   world X [-0.7212, -0.5209]  Y [-0.2273, 0.2273]  Z [0.0488, 0.5198] m
             when seated, i.e. 12.1 mm proud of the fixture top

  With the base down the fixture stands directly on the floor: no stand-off is
  needed, and the module clears it by 48.8 mm.  An earlier placement had the
  fixture inverted and required a 32.1 mm riser to keep the module out of the
  floor; that riser is gone.

  The module starts as a separate free body resting on the work surface, on the
  far side of the base from the fixture so the arm can pick it up and carry it
  across.  Both poses are recorded in cs625_bringup/config/cs625_task_scene.yaml,
  which is the authority for the task; keep the two in step when either changes.
-->
<sdf version="1.9">
  <world name="cs625_insertion_scene">
    <physics name="cs625_task_physics" type="ignored">
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
      <real_time_factor>1.0</real_time_factor>
    </physics>

    <plugin filename="ignition-gazebo-physics-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Physics"/>
    <plugin filename="ignition-gazebo-sensors-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Sensors">
      <render_engine>ogre2</render_engine>
    </plugin>
    <plugin filename="ignition-gazebo-user-commands-system" name="gz::sim::systems::UserCommands"/>
    <plugin filename="ignition-gazebo-scene-broadcaster-system" name="gz::sim::systems::SceneBroadcaster"/>
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-contact-system" name="gz::sim::systems::Contact"/>

    <light type="directional" name="sun">
      <pose>0 0 5 0 0 0</pose>
      <diffuse>1.0 1.0 1.0 1</diffuse>
      <specular>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</specular>
      <direction>-0.5 0.1 -0.9</direction>
    </light>

    <model name="ground_plane">
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>0 0 -0.05 0 0 0</pose>
      <link name="link">
        <collision name="collision">
          <geometry><box><size>10 10 0.1</size></box></geometry>
        </collision>
        <sensor name="task_ground_contact" type="contact">
          <contact><collision>collision</collision></contact>
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>100</update_rate>
          <topic>/cs625_task/contacts/ground</topic>
        </sensor>
        <visual name="visual">
          <geometry><box><size>10 10 0.1</size></box></geometry>
          <material><ambient>0.25 0.25 0.27 1</ambient><diffuse>0.35 0.35 0.38 1</diffuse></material>
        </visual>
      </link>
    </model>

    <!-- Wedge slot fixture, static, in the SolidWorks assembly frame. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://cs625_task/slot_fixture</uri>
      <name>slot_fixture</name>
      <static>true</static>
      <pose>1.030000000 0.570000000 0.290000000 3.110000000 0.080000000 -0.430000000</pose>
    </include>

    <!-- Shielding module, initial pose: resting on the work surface. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://cs625_task/shielding_module</uri>
      <name>shielding_module</name>
      <pose>-0.740000000 -0.970000000 0.230000000 -3.140000000 0.000000000 -0.940000000</pose>
    </include>
  </world>
</sdf>
